--TEST--
ordering two objects reads the class named where the comparison is written: every spelling agrees at the class's own type, and the same two values behind a `mixed` are refused as a catchable throw — `rule:classes/comparable`, `rule:classes/ordering-lowers-to-compare-to`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/comparable
// All five spellings lower to one `compareTo` call at compile time, so the
// class has to be named where the comparison is written. A `mixed` operand
// names none, and `rule:types/arithmetic`'s tag-dispatched ordering has no row
// for an object at all — so a class that *does* implement `Comparable` is
// refused there exactly as a class without it is, and the refusal arrives as a
// throw because the tags are where it first became answerable.
final class Money implements Comparable {
    public function constructor(public int $cents) {}

    public function compareTo(self $other): int {
        return $this->cents <=> $other->cents;
    }
}

final class Loose {
    // Both parameters are `mixed`, so nothing below is a statically typed
    // comparison in disguise.
    public static function order(mixed $a, mixed $b): string {
        try {
            return ($a <=> $b) as string;
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "throws: " . $e->message;
        }
    }
}

var $small = new Money(100);
var $large = new Money(900);

// Typed at its own class, one `compareTo` fixes all five spellings. The count
// is what asserts they agree: an operator that grew a comparison of its own
// fails here while still reading plausibly on any single line.
int $agreed = 0;
if ($small < $large) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($small <= $large) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($large > $small) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($large >= $small) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if (($small <=> $large) < 0) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
echo "agreed ", $agreed as string, "/5\n";

// The same two values, with their class not named at the site.
echo Loose::order($small, $large), "\n";

// The refusal is the ordering operators' and nothing else's: `==` still
// answers, and it stays identity (`rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`).
if ($small == $small) {
    echo "equality still answers\n";
}
var $sameCents = new Money(100);
if ($small != $sameCents) {
    echo "equal contents are not equality\n";
}
// Asking the content question out loud is `compareTo` answering zero.
echo ($small->compareTo($sameCents) == 0) as string, "|\n";
--EXPECT--
agreed 5/5
throws: no ordering for a `object` against a `object` — ordering two objects needs the `Comparable` class named where the comparison is written (`rule:classes/comparable`)
equality still answers
equal contents are not equality
1|
